+  `{:ok, doc}` — see `:value_not_applied` below.
+
+  > #### The value is stored; whether it is *drawn* is a separate question {: .warning}
+  > pdfium writes the field's `/V`. It does **not** regenerate the widget's
+  > `/AP` appearance stream, and `flatten/1` bakes `/AP` while ignoring `/V`. So:
+  >
+  >   * `form_fields/1` reads the new value back, and it survives
+  >     `save_to_bytes/1` — always.
+  >   * `render_page/3` **draws** the new value when pdfium regenerates the
+  >     appearance itself, which it does when the AcroForm sets
+  >     `/NeedAppearances true` or the widget has no `/AP` at all (the common
+  >     case for generated forms). With a stale `/AP` and no `/NeedAppearances`,
+  >     the render shows the **old** text.
+  >   * `flatten/1` bakes the **old** appearance (or nothing) in *both* cases.
+  >     pdfium's form layer builds its appearance for rendering only; it is never
+  >     written back to `/AP`, so flattening never sees it.
+  >
+  > Supplying an appearance stream here would mean font metrics and
+  > content-stream emission, which is beyond a binding, and pdfium's escape hatch
+  > for handing one over (`FPDFAnnot_SetAP`) is not reachable — the underlying
+  > `pdfium-render` crate keeps the raw annotation handle private. If the text
+  > must survive flattening, generate the appearance stream yourself or flatten
+  > with a tool that regenerates appearances. `set_checkbox_value/4` has none of
+  > these caveats.
+
